=
\frac{V_x-12}{2}
+
\frac{V_x-6}{2}\] \[\Rightarrow
\frac{15-V_x}{3}
=
\frac{2V_x-18}{2}\] \[\Rightarrow
V_x=10.5~\text{V}\] Since, \(V_x&lt;12~\text{V},\) Diode \(D_1\) becomes reverse biased and turns OFF. Applying Ohm's law, \[I_x
=
\frac{15-6}{3+2}
=
\frac{9}{5}
=
1.8~\text{mA}\] Hence, \[\boxed{1.8~\text{mA}}\]

Since both op-amps are ideal and operate with negative feedback, <img src="img/adf827caaaedc51f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[V_{-1}=V_{+1}=3\,\text{V},
\qquad
V_{-2}=V_{+2}=2\,\text{V}.\] Let the output of the first op-amp be \(V_{1}\). Applying KCL at the inverting input node of the first op-amp (\(3\,\text{V}\)), \[\frac{3-0}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{3-V_{1}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{3-2}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Simplifying, \[3+(3-V_{1})+1=0,\] \[V_{1}=7\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{7}\]

Since the op-amp is ideal and operates with negative feedback, \[V_{+}=V_{-}=V_{x}.\] <img src="img/b2170cdf9eb12ee9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The inverting input is connected to the output through a voltage divider of \(2\,\text{k}\Omega\) and \(1\,\text{k}\Omega\). Hence, \[V_{-}=\frac{1}{1+2}V_{o}=\frac{V_{o}}{3}.\] Therefore, \[V_{o}=3V_{x}.\] The op-amp input draws zero current. Hence, the input current \(I_{x}\) flows only through the \(8\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor: \[I_{x}=\frac{V_{x}-V_{o}}{8\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{V_{x}-3V_{x}}{8\,\text{k}\Omega}
=-\frac{V_{x}}{4\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Thus, \[\frac{V_{x}}{I_{x}}
=-4\,\text{k}\Omega.\] \[\boxed{-4}\]

The given circuit is a precision half-wave rectifier using an ideal op-amp. The inverting amplifier gain is \[A_v=-\frac{R_f}{R_{in}}
=-\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{20\,\text{k}\Omega}
=-\frac{1}{2}.\] The input is \[v_{in}=2\pi\sin(5000\pi t)\;\text{V},\] whose peak value is \[V_m=2\pi\;\text{V}.\] Hence, the peak output of the rectifier is \[V_{o,m}=\frac{1}{2}(2\pi)=\pi\;\text{V}.\] A PMMC voltmeter with \(1\,\text{Hz}\) bandwidth measures the average (DC) value of the rectified waveform. For a half-wave rectified sine wave, \[V_{\text{avg}}=\frac{V_{o,m}}{\pi}
=\frac{\pi}{\pi}
=1\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{1}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The op-amp and diodes form a precision half-wave rectifier.</li><li>A PMMC instrument measures the average value of the rectified output.</li><li>For a half-wave rectified sine wave, \(V_{\text{avg}}=\dfrac{V_m}{\pi}.\)</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use the RMS value; a PMMC voltmeter reads the average (DC) value.</li></ul></div>

The op-amp works as a charge amplifier with the non-inverting terminal biased at \[V_{ref}=\frac{V_{CC}}{2}=2.5\,\text{V}.\] The feedback capacitor is \[C_f=100\,\text{nF}.\] The piezoelectric sensor generates \[q_p=0.1\sin(10000\pi t)\,\mu\text{C},\] whose peak charge is \[Q_{peak}=0.1\,\mu\text{C}=100\,\text{nC}.\] For a charge amplifier, \[\Delta V_o=\frac{Q_{peak}}{C_f}
=\frac{100\,\text{nC}}{100\,\text{nF}}
=1\,\text{V}.\] Hence, the output swings about the bias voltage as \[V_o=2.5\pm1\,\text{V}.\] Therefore, the maximum (peak) output is \[V_{o,\text{peak}}=2.5+1=3.5\,\text{V}.\] Since the peak detector stores the maximum value, after 10 cycles it reads \[3.5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{3.5}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A charge amplifier converts input charge into voltage using \(V=\dfrac{Q}{C_f}\).</li><li>The sensor capacitances (\(10\,\text{pF}\) and \(100\,\text{pF}\)) do not affect the ideal charge-to-voltage conversion.</li><li>The peak detector stores the maximum output voltage.</li></ul></div>

Given, \[\frac{C(s)}{R(s)}=\frac{1}{1+s}\] For the input \(r(t)=\sin t,\) the excitation frequency is \(\omega=1\) rad/s. Therefore, \[T(j)=\frac{1}{1+j}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\angle-\frac{\pi}{4}\] Hence, the steady-state response is \[c(t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\sin\left(t-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)\] \[\boxed{\text{(D) }\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin\left(t-\dfrac{\pi}{4}\right)}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>If the input to a transfer function \(G(s)\) is \(x(t)=A\sin(\omega_0 t+\phi),\) then the steady-state output is: \[y(t)=A|G(j\omega_0)|\sin\!\left(\omega_0 t+\phi+\angle G(j\omega_0)\right)\]</li></ul></div>

The transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{1+0.2s}{1+0.05s}\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1</div>Compare with the standard phase lead network \[G(s)=\frac{1+sT}{1+s\alpha T},\qquad \alpha&lt;1.\] Here, \(T=0.2\) and \(\alpha=\dfrac{0.05}{0.2}=0.25\) The frequency at maximum phase lead is \[\omega_m=\frac{1}{T\sqrt{\alpha}}
=\frac{1}{0.2\times0.5}
=10~\mathrm{rad/s}\]</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2</div>The zero frequency is \(\omega_z=\dfrac{1}{0.2}=5~\mathrm{rad/s}\) The pole frequency is \(\omega_p=\dfrac{1}{0.05}=20~\mathrm{rad/s}\) The maximum phase shift occurs at the geometric mean of the zero and pole frequencies. \[\omega_m=\sqrt{\omega_z\omega_p}
=\sqrt{5\times20}
=10~\mathrm{rad/s}\]</div> \[\boxed{(A)\;10~\mathrm{rad/s}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a phase lead network, the maximum phase lead occurs at the geometric mean of the zero and pole frequencies.</li><li>The formula is \(\omega_m=\dfrac{1}{T\sqrt{\alpha}},~~\alpha&lt;1.\)</li></ul></div>
